			<section class="book-page" id="overview-1">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Decoding the Burn</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Executive Summary &bull; Page 1 of 2</span>
					</div>

					<h2 class="chapter-title" style="margin-top: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">Executive Summary: Overview &amp; Foundations</h2>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<div class="exec-summary-wrap">
							<p class="exec-lead-paragraph">
								Burning Mouth Pain (often labeled as Burning Mouth Syndrome, and commonly experienced by patients across several related conditions) presents a difficult clinical puzzle because standard visual examinations of the oral mucosa typically appear completely normal. While chronic oral burning can involve complex neurological or systemic factors, there is emerging scientific research examining how oral acidity, acid-sensing ion channels, and subtle dental micro-pathways interact. This book explores that research, offering an observational, first-principles framework to help patients and clinicians investigate possible chemical and mechanical contributors alongside established clinical&nbsp;care.
							</p>

							<div class="exec-section-card">
								<h3 class="exec-card-title">Part I: Understanding Burning Mouth Pain</h3>
								<p class="exec-card-text">
									A thorough look at the epidemiological realities, daily symptom patterns, and heavy psychological toll of chronic oral burning. We examine why clean visual exams and standard blood panels can leave patients feeling stranded, and how systematic observational tracking establishes a clearer starting&nbsp;point.
								</p>
							</div>

							<div class="exec-section-card">
								<h3 class="exec-card-title">Part II: My Story (An N=1 Investigation)</h3>
								<p class="exec-card-text">
									A personal, chronological case history documenting a software engineer's sudden onset of 10/10 burning pain after consuming acidic craft beer and wine. It details the exhausting cycle of clean dental radiographs, misattributions, and the rigorous trial-and-error that revealed a 48-hour delayed reaction to dietary acids and fermentable&nbsp;starches.
								</p>
							</div>

							<div class="exec-section-card">
								<h3 class="exec-card-title">Part III: The First Principles of Oral Burning Pain</h3>
								<p class="exec-card-text">
									The biophysical and receptor mechanisms at the heart of the burn. We examine how subtle enamel wear, gumline recession, and crown micro-margins leave microscopic dentinal tubules patent, allowing ambient dietary protons (H<sup>+</sup>) to gate Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC) and sensitize unmyelinated trigeminal&nbsp;nociceptors.
								</p>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>vi</span>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY: PAGE 2 (Page vii) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="overview-2">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Decoding the Burn</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Executive Summary &bull; Page 2 of 2</span>
					</div>

					<h2 class="chapter-title" style="margin-top: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">Executive Summary: Protocols &amp; Collaboration</h2>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<div class="exec-summary-wrap">
							<div class="exec-section-card">
								<h3 class="exec-card-title">Part IV: The BMS Diagnostic History</h3>
								<p class="exec-card-text">
									A historical review tracing the shift from psychiatric stigmatization to modern neuro-histological validation. We explore international consensus criteria (ICOP 2020), small-fiber neuropathy diagnostics, and the distinction between primary idiopathic presentations and secondary mechanical&nbsp;triggers.
								</p>
							</div>

							<div class="exec-section-card">
								<h3 class="exec-card-title">Part V: Taking Charge of Our Own Investigation</h3>
								<p class="exec-card-text">
									Practical citizen science tools: self-assessment quizzes, symptom ledgers, latency mapping, and barrier protocols (tubule desensitizers, cervical composite shields). Crucially, this part concludes with what to do if acid reduction does not bring relief, providing data on alternative evidence-based therapies known to help in non-acid&nbsp;cases.
								</p>
							</div>

							<div class="exec-section-card">
								<h3 class="exec-card-title">Part VI: Models for BMS, Healthcare Dialogue &amp; Citizen Science</h3>
								<p class="exec-card-text">
									Constructive communication strategies for partnering with dentists and medical doctors. By comparing BMS to the diabetic small-fiber neuropathy model, patients can use objective data to overcome systemic clinical bottlenecks and advocate effectively for personalized&nbsp;care.
								</p>
							</div>

							<div class="exec-takeaway-box">
								<div class="exec-takeaway-title">The Core Takeaway</div>
								<p class="exec-takeaway-text">
									Nerve pathways transmit the pain, but ambient oral acidity and compromised physical barriers frequently act as peripheral triggers. Exploring acid reduction and dental margin integrity provides a safe, constructive path forward without dismissing alternative clinical&nbsp;causes.
								</p>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>vii</span>
				</div>
