					<h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 2: What Sufferers Experience: Symptoms &amp; Realities</h2>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<div id="ch2-symptoms" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
							<h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: #7c3aed; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">Symptoms</h3>
							<p>Burning Mouth Syndrome presents with a relentless clinical triad. The predominant symptom is a bilateral, continuous scalding sensation across the oral mucosal surface, frequently accompanied by phantom metallic or bitter taste disturbances (dysgeusia) and subjective dry mouth (xerostomia) despite normal salivary flow. Crucially, the oral mucosa appears completely healthy and pink under examination, leaving clinicians without an observable structural target on visual inspection while small-fiber nerve endings fire&nbsp;continuously.</p>
						</div>

						<div id="ch2-where" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
							<h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: #7c3aed; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">Where It Hurts</h3>
							<p>The pain maps strictly to sensory pathways of the trigeminal nerve (V3 and V2 branches). The anterior two-thirds and tip of the tongue are affected in over 85% of cases via the lingual nerve. The lateral tongue borders, anterior hard palate, and mucosal surface of the lower lip are secondary hot zones. Notably, the floor of the mouth and buccal mucosa are typically spared, reflecting specific receptor densities and hardware contact areas.</p>
						</div>

						<div id="ch2-when" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
							<h3 style="font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: #7c3aed; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">When It Hurts</h3>
							<p>The pain follows a characteristic diurnal crescendo: patients wake with mild or nonexistent discomfort, but burning steadily intensifies through late morning, reaching peak agony in late afternoon and evening. Strikingly, sufferers report temporary relief while eating or chewing, because mastication stimulates bicarbonate-rich salivary flow that temporarily washes protons away from mucosal acid sensors.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="voice-showcase-wrap" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
							<div class="voice-short-item">
								<p class="voice-short-quote">
									&ldquo;It feels like I drank a scalding cup of boiling coffee every morning, and the burn never goes&nbsp;away.&rdquo;
								</p>
								<div class="voice-short-name">Sarah M.</div>
							</div>

							<div class="voice-short-item">
								<p class="voice-short-quote">
									&ldquo;Three specialists smiled and told me everything looked fine while I sat in the chair holding back&nbsp;tears.&rdquo;
								</p>
								<div class="voice-short-name">Claire D.</div>
							</div>
						</div>
