						<div id="ch6-spotless-labs" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The "Spotless Lab Paradox" &amp; The Specialist&nbsp;Runaround</h4>
						<p>When the pain flared uncontrollably between dietary changes, I consulted primary care physicians, ENTs, and neurologists. Every standard diagnostic test came back completely normal: complete blood counts, ferritin, vitamin B12, autoimmune panels (ANA, Anti-SSA/SSB), inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR), and brain MRI and head CT scans. To modern medicine, I was the picture of perfect health, yet my mouth felt like it was immersed in boiling&nbsp;oil.</p>
						<p>Because physical inspection reveals intact pink mucosa and routine systemic labs return normal, clinical guidelines direct specialists toward central neuromodulators: clonazepam, gabapentin, or amitriptyline. These medications are designed to dial down central gain and dampen dorsal horn wind-up. While they provide meaningful coping relief for patients with intense central amplification, they are not engineered to detect or seal peripheral micro-chemical leaks at the tooth&nbsp;margin.</p>

