						<h3>Episode #3: Routine Dental Cleaning (Mechanical &amp; Bacterial&nbsp;Shock)</h3>
						<p>Believing my oral hygiene might be contributing, I scheduled a routine dental prophylaxis. The hygienist performed standard ultrasonic scaling and debridement, followed by an antimicrobial chlorhexidine rinse. Within 48 hours, my mouth erupted into an intense 10/10 flare. This was not a reflection of hygiene technique, but of my underlying tissue vulnerability: ultrasonic vibrations can temporarily disrupt a fragile salivary pellicle on unshielded roots, while low-pH or alcohol-containing rinses can irritate exposed dentinal tubules and lower the firing threshold of dormant, sensitized nerve&nbsp;endings.</p>
