						<div id="ep4-fruit" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h3>Episode #4: Identifying Direct Chemical Irritants (The High-Acid Fruit&nbsp;Phase)</h3>
						<p>Recognizing that dietary acids directly reignited mucosal burning, I began testing specific foods. While fresh fruits and acidic vegetables are nutrient-dense and healthy for normal oral mucosa, they act as acute topical irritants when dentinal tubules and mucosal barriers are unshielded. Citrus fruits, berries, pineapples, tomatoes, and apples provoked intense burning within 2 to 4 hours of ingestion. Temporarily eliminating high-acid fruits reduced my baseline pain from an 8-10/10 down to a manageable 2-3/10, providing conclusive proof that organic fruit acids act as direct chemical irritants on sensitized oral&nbsp;tissues.</p>
						<p style="margin-top: 0.4rem; padding: 0.35rem 0.65rem; background: rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #10b981; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.86rem; color: #065f46;">
							<strong>Pain Level Outcome:</strong> Pain dropped from 8-10/10 down to a manageable <strong>2-3/10</strong> baseline.
						</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The Biochemical Mechanism of Fruit Acid&nbsp;Flares</h4>
						<p>Unlike whole proteins or neutral fats, fruits deliver concentrated solutions of free citric, malic, and tartaric acids directly across the oral mucosa. In a healthy mouth, thick salivary glycoproteins buffer these organic acids within minutes. But in BMS patients, where the protective epithelial barrier is micro-compromised and salivary buffering is depressed, hydrogen ions (protons) rapidly penetrate into the sub-epithelial layer, directly depolarizing acid-sensing ion channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3) on raw lingual nerve&nbsp;terminals.</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The Fruit Elimination &amp; Challenge&nbsp;Protocol</h4>
						<p>To distinguish between a true systemic food allergy and direct chemical acid excitation, I conducted systematic single-variable challenge tests. Neutral plant foods with a pH above 6.0 (such as cucumber, peeled zucchini, and steamed cauliflower) produced zero flare-up. But re-introducing even a quarter of an apple or a single strawberry ignited the signature burning scald within 120 minutes. This confirmed that pH and acid chemistry, not botanical species or protein antigens, was the active pharmacological trigger.</p>
