						<h4 class="episode-subheading">The 72-Hour Latency Trap &amp; The 1-Year Yo-Yo&nbsp;Dance</h4>
						<p>Whenever I attempted to reintroduce carbohydrates (such as rice, potatoes, or sourdough bread), I fell into what I call the <strong>72-Hour Latency Trap</strong>. Unlike an allergic reaction that triggers hives within minutes, oral bacterial fermentation produces a delayed response curve. Eating carbs on Monday night fed bacterial biofilms, which generated lactic acid through Tuesday, finally breaching dentinal tubules and igniting a 10/10 nerve flare on Wednesday or&nbsp;Thursday.</p>
						<p>Because of this 48 to 72 hour time lag, I spent an entire year in a bewildering "yo-yo dance," falsely blaming whatever I ate on Thursday for a fire that was actually ignited by carbohydrates consumed three days earlier. Only meticulous daily tracking revealed the mathematical latency.</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">Functional Cellular B12 &amp; Lingual Nerve&nbsp;Myelination</h4>
						<p>During this period, research in neuro-metabolism demonstrated that routine serum B12 blood tests primarily measure circulating total vitamin, which can mask functional cellular deficiency. Optimizing cellular B12 (sublingual methylcobalamin with adenosylcobalamin) and keeping homocysteine below 8.0 &mu;mol/L provided the biochemical raw materials required for Schwann cells to remyelinate damaged lingual nerve fibers once acid exposure was halted.</p>
