					<div class="chapter-content">
						<div id="ep9-gluma" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h3>Episode #9: Gluma Tubule Desensitizer on Exposed&nbsp;Margins</h3>
						<p>After excavating decay and placing a monolithic zirconia crown with hermetic resin bonding, 95% of my oral burning vanished permanently. However, mild sensitivity persisted along two exposed cervical root margins. Last week, I applied <strong>Gluma Desensitizer</strong> (a clinical formulation of glutaraldehyde and HEMA) directly to those exposed root surfaces.</p>
						<p>The glutaraldehyde immediately cross-linked amino acids in the dentinal tubule fluid, precipitating plasma proteins and forming a physical plug inside the dentin tubules. Within seconds, the remaining burning sensation vanished completely. This final intervention provided absolute pharmacological proof: blocking dentinal fluid movement stops peripheral nerve firing instantly.</p>
						<p style="margin-top: 0.4rem; padding: 0.35rem 0.65rem; background: rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #10b981; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.86rem; color: #065f46;">
							<strong>Pain Level Outcome:</strong> Immediate drop from 2-3/10 post-meal tingling to a complete, stable <strong>0/10</strong>.
						</p>

						<h4 class="episode-subheading">Core Engineering Takeaways from Four Years of N=1&nbsp;Tracking</h4>
						<p>My four-year journey from debilitating 10/10 pain to complete, lasting remission established three undeniable clinical truths:</p>
						<ul class="chapter-list">
							<li><strong>Peripheral Hardware Leaks Drive Mucosal Burning:</strong> BMS is not a psychosomatic illusion or an incurable central brain glitch. It is driven by tangible peripheral triggers, specifically unsealed dental restorations and exposed cervical dentin.</li>
							<li><strong>Acid Is the Fuel, Bacteria Are the Engine, Tubules Are the Conduit:</strong> Eliminating dietary acids and fermentable carbohydrates removes the chemical fuel that drives intradental nerve depolarization.</li>
							<li><strong>"Idiopathic" Is an Institutional Artifact:</strong> Pain is only labeled idiopathic when clinicians lack the tools, time, or interdisciplinary training to locate the peripheral leak.</li>
						</ul>
						<p style="margin-top: 0.6rem;">With the clinical proof of my N=1 journey established, Part III breaks down the first principles of oral biophysics, receptor pharmacology, and trigeminal neuroanatomy that govern why oral burning occurs.</p>
