					<h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 10: Identify What Makes Pain&nbsp;Worse</h2>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>Three years into my burning mouth, I asked myself a simple first-principle question: What makes my pain worse and what makes it&nbsp;better?</p>
						<p>For me, after I've tried the carnivore diet, I realized that everything that I've done so far was to remove some type of acid. It's at the beginning it was beer, and then coffee, and then fruits, and then acidic stuff, and then vegetables which have sugar which transform into&nbsp;acid.</p>
						<p>And for the dentist work that I have done, it was to block the tubules, which basically are triggered by acid as well. When you inspect an exposed tooth neck or a microscopic gap beneath an aging crown, dentin is not a solid wall. It is perforated by 20,000 to 45,000 microscopic fluid-filled pipes per square millimeter, known as dentinal tubules. When direct acids from beverages enter those open channels, they pull fluid outward via capillary pressure and wash hydrogen protons directly against the pulpal nerve&nbsp;endings.</p>
						<p>Even more deceptive is the mechanism of fermentable sugar. When sweet liquids, fruits, or refined starches pass into the mouth, microscopic sugars seep into open tubules and gumline margins. Oral biofilm bacteria (<em>Streptococcus mutans</em> and <em>Lactobacillus</em>) feed on those sugars, fermenting them into concentrated lactic and acetic acids. For 48 to 72 hours after a single sugary snack, oral bacteria continue pumping fresh acid directly against open tooth&nbsp;conduits.</p>
						<p>Since 2014, science understands the clear mechanism of ASICs (Acid-Sensing Ion Channels). Pioneering crystal structure work published in <em>Nature</em> in 2014 resolved the atomic architecture of these channels, proving that extracellular hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>) bind directly to specialized pockets on sensory nerve terminals. When oral pH drops below neutral, ASICs spring open, driving an inward flood of sodium ions that forces sensory nerves to fire high-frequency burning pain directly into the trigeminal&nbsp;pathway.</p>

						<h3>The Progression</h3>
						<p>Recognizing the acid connection did not happen overnight. It unfolded through a relentless, multi-year process of progressive elimination. At first, I had no theoretical framework. I simply noticed patterns in my daily tracking journal: whenever I drank beer, my mouth flared violently. Beer carries a triple insult: high acidity (pH 4.0 to 4.5), effervescent carbonation, and ethanol, which dehydrates the mucosal lining and strips away the protective salivary mucin&nbsp;blanket.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="page-number-footer">
					<span>28</span>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Chapter 10: Identify What Makes Pain Worse (Page 2 of 3) -->

			<section class="book-page" id="page-29">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Part III &bull; Chapter 10: Identify What Makes Pain Worse</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 29</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<p>Cutting beer was my first breakthrough. Once beer was gone, my peak pain flares dampened slightly, but persistent burning remained. That led me to examine my morning ritual: coffee. Coffee is packed with chlorogenic, caffeic, and citric acids, measuring between pH 4.8 and 5.2. Every morning, I was effectively bathing my sensitized nerve endings in warm acid. Eliminating coffee brought another noticeable drop in daily nerve&nbsp;irritation.</p>
						<p>Next came fruits. Sufferers are often told to eat more fruit for health, yet citrus, berries, apples, and tomatoes deliver intense doses of citric, malic, and ascorbic acids. Furthermore, fruit delivers concentrated fructose, which fuels subgingival bacterial fermentation. Eliminating fruit lowered my baseline burn even&nbsp;further.</p>
						<p>Finally, I stripped away acidic condiments, salad dressings, vinegars, and then vegetables containing fermentable carbohydrates that oral bacteria convert into acid. When every dietary acid and fermentable carb was eliminated on an all-meat carnivore protocol, the relentless fire in my mouth quieted down to a stable zero out of&nbsp;ten.</p>

						<h3>The Community Data: Proof That I Am Not the Only&nbsp;One</h3>
						<p>Discovering that eliminating acid eliminated my pain was life-changing, but I needed to know whether my recovery was an isolated personal fluke or a shared biological reality across the wider Burning Mouth Syndrome&nbsp;community.</p>
						<p>To find out, I ran a targeted poll inside our Burning Mouth Syndrome Facebook support community, asking fellow sufferers: <em>"I am curious to know how many of us have noticed that acidic foods and drinks (coffee, wine, soft drinks, tomato sauce, citrus) make the burning&nbsp;worse."</em></p>
						<p>Seventy-seven patients participated, and the community data proved conclusively that <strong>I am not the only&nbsp;one:</strong></p>
						<p><strong>75% Answered YES (58 of 77 participants):</strong> Three out of every four patients confirmed that acidic foods and beverages directly worsen or ignite their oral&nbsp;burning.</p>
						<p><strong>16% Answered NO (12 participants):</strong> Did not observe an obvious link between dietary acid and their symptom&nbsp;spikes.</p>
						<p><strong>9% Answered OTHER (7 participants):</strong> Noted mixed observations, delayed 48-hour flares, or had not tracked food acidity&nbsp;yet.</p>
						<figure class="book-figure">
							<img src="/facebook-poll-acidity-trigger.png" alt="Facebook Community Poll Results showing 75% of 77 BMS participants confirming acidity makes burning worse" loading="lazy" />
							<figcaption class="book-figure-caption">
								<strong>Figure 3: Community Acidity Poll (n=77).</strong> Three out of four Burning Mouth Syndrome patients report that acidic foods and beverages directly intensify their oral&nbsp;burning.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
