					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>Why Eliminating Acid Reveals What Makes It&nbsp;Better</h3>
						<p>If something makes the pain worse, can doing less of what makes it worse reduce the pain below your baseline? That's the question I asked myself. It was a question that I had, and it seems that it worked for&nbsp;me.</p>
						<p>I am looking for other people who have tried that and getting some types of general stats on this. In engineering and clinical troubleshooting, identifying the primary amplifier of a signal is half the solution. If pouring acid on sensitized nerve endings and exposed tooth conduits drives an intense flare, then completely removing that acid input removes the continuous chemical stimulus. Doing less of what harms the tissue gives peripheral receptors their first genuine opportunity to rest, desensitize, and return to a quiescent baseline&nbsp;state.</p>
						<p>In our community tracking survey, patient reported triggers fell consistently into three distinct biological&nbsp;categories:</p>
						<p><strong>1. Direct Chemical Acids:</strong> Citrus juices (pH 2.0 to 3.5), wine and beer (tartaric, malic, and carbonic acids), coffee (pH 4.8 to 5.2), vinegars (pH 2.4 to 3.4), carbonated sparkling waters, and commercial mouthwashes. These liquids immediately wash free protons across sensitized oral&nbsp;tissues.</p>
						<p><strong>2. Fermentable Sugars and Starches:</strong> Bread, pasta, crackers, sweets, chocolate, and sugary vegetables like carrots, beets, and potatoes. While these items do not taste sour, resident oral bacteria rapidly ferment trapped residues into concentrated lactic acid directly against gingival margins and open tubules for up to 72&nbsp;hours.</p>
						<p><strong>3. Secondary Receptor Agonists:</strong> Spicy capsaicin (chili peppers), menthol in toothpastes, and high-sodium foods that dehydrate fragile epithelial&nbsp;cells.</p>

