					<h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 11: Where Are the Acid-Sensing Receptors in the&nbsp;Mouth?</h2>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>When sufferers describe burning mouth pain, clinicians frequently examine the oral tissues and find no macroscopic ulcers or erythema. This paradox leads many to mistakenly conclude that the pain lacks a physical basis. However, modern sensory neurobiology reveals that the human mouth is not a passive lining. It is an intensely wired sensor array packed with specialized <strong>Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASICs)</strong> that monitor biochemical changes in real&nbsp;time.</p>

						<figure class="book-figure">
							<img src="/oral-cavity-asic-receptor-density-map.jpg" alt="3D Anatomical Map of Acid-Sensing Receptors in the Human Oral Cavity showing density in dental pulp, tongue, and palate" loading="lazy" />
							<figcaption class="book-figure-caption">
								<strong>Figure 1: Oral Acid-Sensing Receptor Density Map.</strong> Human sensory nerve terminals in the oral cavity carry up to 50 times higher nociceptive density than regular body skin. Key anatomical concentrations include:
								<strong>Dental Pulp:</strong> ~1,000+ nerve terminals/mm&sup2;; <strong>Tongue (Lingual Surface):</strong> ~200 to 500 terminals/mm&sup2;; <strong>Hard Palate (Roof of Mouth):</strong> ~100 to 250 terminals/mm&sup2;; <strong>Gingival Sulcus &amp; Buccal Mucosa:</strong> ~50 to 100 terminals/mm&sup2;; compared to <strong>Body Skin (Arms &amp; Legs):</strong> only ~10 to 20 terminals/mm&sup2;.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<p>As mapped in Figure 1, the oral cavity possesses one of the highest concentrations of sensory nerve endings anywhere in the human body. The dental pulp alone contains over 1,000 nerve terminals per square millimeter, while the anterior tongue and hard palate carry 200 to 500 terminals per square millimeter. When microscopic acid breaches protective barriers, it does not encounter inert skin. It directly washes across millions of unmyelinated trigeminal nerve endings primed to trigger excruciating burning&nbsp;alarms.</p>
					</div>
				</div>

