						<h3>Factor 4: Compounding Autoimmune and Systemic&nbsp;Vulnerabilities</h3>
						<p>This structural crisis is frequently amplified by systemic health differences that disproportionately affect&nbsp;women:</p>
						<p><strong>Autoimmune Infiltration:</strong> Women represent roughly 80% of all autoimmune patients. Autoimmune conditions such as Sj&ouml;gren's syndrome (which has a 9:1 female ratio) and Hashimoto's thyroiditis (8:1 female ratio) cause silent, progressive lymphocytic destruction of salivary gland tissue long before full clinical diagnosis, stripping the mouth of its protective fluid&nbsp;barrier.</p>
						<p><strong>Ferritin and Iron Depletion:</strong> Decades of menstrual blood loss frequently leave women entering mid-life with depleted ferritin stores. Iron is an essential cofactor for oral epithelial maintenance and nerve energy metabolism; low ferritin impairs mucosal repair and lowers neuropathic pain&nbsp;thresholds.</p>
						<p><strong>Polypharmacy Side Effects:</strong> Women in their forties and fifties are statistically prescribed higher rates of SSRIs, SNRIs, blood pressure medications, and antihistamines. Over four hundred commonly prescribed drugs cause dry mouth as a known side effect, compounding the salivary deficit into a severe, chronic&nbsp;vulnerability.</p>

