					<h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 22: Calculating Your&nbsp;Time&nbsp;Lag</h2>

					<div class="chapter-content is-first-page">
						<p>The single greatest obstacle preventing patients and doctors from solving Burning Mouth Syndrome is the deceptive phenomenon of <strong>delayed chemical latency</strong>. We are biologically conditioned to look for immediate cause and effect: if a wasp stings your finger, it swells in seconds; if you eat a peanut you are allergic to, your throat tightens in&nbsp;minutes.</p>
						<p>In oral neuropathic pain, however, the chemical fuse burns on a <strong>48 to 72 hour time lag</strong>. Once you have established a calm baseline by starving the fire, you can test suspect foods and drinks systematically. To do that accurately, you must understand how the biological clock unfolds in three distinct&nbsp;stages:</p>
						<h3>The 72-Hour Latency Wave: Why Monday's Drink Burns on&nbsp;Wednesday</h3>
						<p><strong>1. The Ingress Phase (Hour 0 to 6):</strong> You drink a cup of coffee, a glass of wine, or a sparkling seltzer. The acidic liquid washes over exposed root collars or seeps beneath the margins of a crown with early cement washout. Initial burning may feel minimal because saliva briefly buffers the&nbsp;surface.</p>
						<p><strong>2. The Microbial Fermentation Phase (Hour 12 to 36):</strong> Microscopic food starches and fermentable carbohydrates remain trapped inside subgingival pockets and porous dentin tubules. Anaerobic oral bacteria feast on these residues, continuously manufacturing concentrated lactic acid right at the nerve&nbsp;threshold.</p>
						<p><strong>3. The ASIC Receptor Wind-Up (Hour 36 to 72):</strong> The local tissue pH steadily declines below 6.5. Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3) lock wide open, causing a massive influx of sodium and calcium ions. Sensory C-fibers depolarize relentlessly, triggering central sensitization in the spinal trigeminal nucleus. By Wednesday afternoon, your tongue scalds at an 8 out&nbsp;of&nbsp;10.</p>
