						<h3>3. How can an exposed margin on a single lower molar burn the roof of the mouth and the tip of the&nbsp;tongue?</h3>
						<p>This is the single hardest concept for conventional doctors and dentists to understand. Clinicians are taught that dental pain is localized: an exposed root on tooth #36 (lower left first molar) or tooth #26 (upper left first molar) should produce a sharp one-second zinger in that specific tooth, not a diffuse scald across the anterior palate or tongue tip.</p>
						<p>The bridge between a localized tooth leak and widespread whole-mouth fire is governed by the neuroanatomy of <strong>Spatial Summation</strong> and <strong>Convergence-Projection</strong> in the brainstem:</p>
						<p><strong>Convergence onto Wide Dynamic Range (WDR) Neurons:</strong> Primary pulpal and periodontal sensory afferents from the Mandibular branch (V<sub>3</sub>) and Maxillary branch (V<sub>2</sub>) do not route into isolated private wires in the brainstem. Instead, thousands of individual tooth and tissue fibers converge onto shared, multi-receptive Wide Dynamic Range (WDR) second-order neurons inside the <em>Subnucleus Caudalis</em> (the medullary dorsal horn).</p>
						<p><strong>Glial Pump Overload:</strong> When a leaky crown margin or receded gumline leaves hundreds of thousands of fluid-filled dentinal tubules open to everyday dietary acids and bacterial fermentation, living pulpal C-fibers fire continuous, high-frequency action potentials 24 hours a day. In the synapse, this torrential bombardment releases massive volumes of excitatory glutamate and Substance P, completely overwhelming local glial astrocytic reuptake pumps (GLT-1 and EAAT2).</p>
						<p><strong>Volume Transmission (Neurotransmitter Spillover):</strong> Because astrocytic reuptake pumps cannot clear the flooded synapse, excess glutamate physically spills out of the synaptic cleft into adjacent neural columns. This phenomenon, known as <em>Volume Transmission</em>, bathes neighboring dormant synapses that process telemetry from the anterior tongue (lingual nerve) and hard palate (nasopalatine nerve).</p>
						<p><strong>Cortical Mismapping:</strong> When these neighboring WDR neurons depolarize from the chemical spillover, they transmit emergency pain signals up the spinothalamic tract to the primary somatosensory cortex (S1). Because the cerebral cortex receives a massive convergent flood across shared second-order pathways, it cannot resolve the origin to an isolated molar margin: it maps the alarm across the entire anterior oral&nbsp;vault.</p>

						<div id="ch31-mastication" style="scroll-margin-top: 5rem;"></div>
						<h3>4. Why does burning vanish while eating, but return when you&nbsp;stop?</h3>
						<p>A frequent and understandable objection is: <em>"We are all different. I can eat anything, and when I eat, my symptoms vanish, but return when I stop. How could my burning possibly be related to food or&nbsp;acid?"</em></p>
						<p>First and foremost, <strong>we are all different</strong>. Burning mouth pain can stem from primary central neuropathies, autoimmune salivary conditions (like Sj&ouml;gren's), or hematological deficits where dietary chemistry plays a minimal role. However, when eating causes symptoms to temporarily vanish, it does not disprove an acid connection. In fact, it is explained by three powerful neuro-physiological&nbsp;mechanisms:</p>
						<p><strong>1. Gate Control Theory (Sensory Overdrive):</strong> The moment you chew, high-threshold pressure receptors, periodontal ligament mechanoreceptors, and mucosal touch fibers fire thousands of high-velocity signals along thick, myelinated A&beta; nerve fibers into the brainstem (<em>Subnucleus Caudalis</em>). This rapid wave of mechanical input activates inhibitory interneurons, effectively slamming shut the neural pain gate. For the 15 to 20 minutes you chew, the brainstem literally cannot process the slow, unmyelinated C-fiber burning alarm.</p>
						<p><strong>2. The Alkaline Bicarbonate Flood:</strong> Under resting conditions, saliva flow trickles at a modest 0.3 to 0.4 mL/min with minimal buffering power (pH ~6.5), allowing ambient acids to cling to exposed dentinal tubules and mucosal nerve endings. Chewing food triggers immediate parasympathetic stimulation of the parotid and submandibular salivary glands, multiplying saliva volume up to 2.5 to 3.0 mL/min and increasing bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) concentrations by over 400%. This rush of alkaline saliva (pH 7.8 to 8.2) instantly washes the mouth, neutralizing surface protons and closing proton-gated <strong>Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3)</strong>.</p>
						<p><strong>3. Physical Coating and Bolus Shielding:</strong> Chewed food, mixed with thick salivary mucins and dietary fats, forms a temporary physical insulating barrier over the tongue, gums, and exposed tooth roots. This blocks evaporative cooling, air friction, and resting tissue&nbsp;exposure.</p>
						<p><strong>Why the Burn Returns Shortly After You Stop:</strong> Within 15 to 30 minutes of swallowing your last bite, stimulated salivary flow plummets back to resting drought levels, the A&beta; mechanical gate re-opens, and the protective coating clears. More importantly, microscopic food residues trapped between teeth and along crown margins begin fermenting into bacterial lactic acid, reigniting the burn on a delayed 24-to-72-hour curve.</p>
						<p>If you eliminate dietary acids and fermentable carbohydrates for 2 to 3 weeks and notice zero shift in your baseline pain, your case may truly be independent of food triggers. But do not let temporary relief <em>during</em> meals trick you into dismissing oral&nbsp;chemistry.</p>
