						<h3>5. Why do some people have little or no pain in the morning, and it steadily progresses during the&nbsp;day?</h3>
						<p>This diurnal progression is one of the classic, textbook hallmarks of Burning Mouth Syndrome (traditionally categorized as <em>Type 1 BMS</em>). Sufferers wake up with completely calm or minimal symptoms, only to watch the fire steadily build across the afternoon, peaking between 5:00 PM and 9:00 PM. This progression is driven by three compounding daytime mechanisms:</p>
						<p><strong>Cumulative Chemical Exposure and Proton Loading:</strong> During 7 to 8 hours of sleep, zero dietary acids or carbohydrates enter the mouth. Oral tissues experience chemical rest. But as the day unfolds, every cup of coffee (pH 5.0), fruit, acidic vinaigrette, and fermentable snack delivers a fresh wash of hydrogen protons (H<sup>+</sup>). Subgingival bacteria ferment trapped food residues, generating localized pools of lactic acid against unshielded tooth roots and leaking crowns. These protons dock into <strong>Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3)</strong>, firing high-voltage nerve telemetry that compounds with each meal.</p>
						<p><strong>Glial Saturation and Synaptic "Wind-Up":</strong> At night, second-order sensory neurons in the brainstem (<em>Subnucleus Caudalis</em>) rest, allowing glial astrocytic reuptake pumps (GLT-1 and EAAT2) to fully clear residual excitatory neurotransmitters. But as peripheral nerves fire continuous action potentials hour after hour during the day, the synaptic cleft is flooded with glutamate and Substance P. By late afternoon, glial reuptake pumps reach maximum capacity and become overwhelmed. Excess glutamate pools in the synapse, creating <strong>temporal summation (central wind-up)</strong>: the sensory threshold plummets, turning routine signals into an unbearable evening scald.</p>
						<p><strong>Mechanical Shearing and Speech Micro-Friction:</strong> The human tongue swallows roughly 800 times a day and moves constantly during speech. As the day unfolds, thousands of micro-friction cycles drag the delicate lingual mucosa across sharp tooth edges, thinned enamel, or unpolished crown margins. On healthy tissue, this friction is imperceptible; on a sensitized, hyper-innervated oral mucosa, mechanical contact is perceived as burning heat (allodynia). When you fall asleep, the mouth rests, the synapse clears, and the circuit resets for the next morning.</p>
					</div>
