						<h3>6. Why do my dentist and dental X-rays repeatedly insist that everything is "completely&nbsp;normal"?</h3>
						<p>Dental education is rigorously structured around identifying macroscopic structural disease: interproximal caries, crestal bone loss, structural fractures, or periapical radiolucencies on diagnostic radiographs.</p>
						<p>However, two-dimensional bitewing radiographs cannot penetrate radiopaque metal, gold, or dense zirconia crowns; the restoration casts an opaque silhouette that physically limits radiographic assessment of early cement luting interface degradation along internal prep walls. In addition, standard tactile dental explorers cannot detect sub-micron fluid micro-leakage or exposed dentinal tubules (which require scanning electron microscopy). When clinical probing and radiographs reveal intact bone and absence of gross decay, the teeth are appropriately judged structurally sound by standard restorative criteria, even while localized acidic micro-environments continue to stimulate adjacent unmyelinated pulpal and mucosal&nbsp;afferents.</p>

						<h3>7. Is Burning Mouth Syndrome really in my head or caused by anxiety and&nbsp;depression?</h3>
						<p>No. Historically, because macroscopic mucosal exams appear pristine, BMS was frequently categorized as a somatoform disorder or functional neurosis. That paradigm was permanently revised in 2005 when Dr. Giuseppe Lauria and colleagues published definitive histological punch biopsy evidence in <em>Pain</em> <a href="#src-9" class="cite-ref">[9]</a>.</p>
						<p>Microscopic evaluation of the anterior tongue proved that BMS patients suffer from genuine, objective <strong>small-fiber trigeminal neuropathy</strong>: a quantifiable loss of intraepithelial nerve fibers paired with an abnormal upregulation of TRPV1 heat and acid receptors. Anxiety, sleep disruption, and depression are the natural secondary psychological consequences of enduring 24/7 unremitting physical pain without medical validation, not the causative origin.</p>

						<h3>8. How long does it realistically take for oral nerves to calm down once acid and triggers are&nbsp;eliminated?</h3>
						<p>Healing follows a predictable three-phase biological timeline once physical leaks are sealed and dietary acid is removed:</p>
						<p><strong>Phase 1 (Days 1 to 3): Chemical Substrate Starvation:</strong> Transitioning to a zero-acid or carnivore baseline deprives oral biofilm bacteria of fermentable starches, dropping subgingival lactic acid production toward zero within 48 to 72 hours.</p>
						<p><strong>Phase 2 (Days 7 to 14): Receptor De-escalation:</strong> As oral pH normalizes to neutral 7.0-7.4, free hydrogen protons clear from extracellular fluid, allowing Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC1 and ASIC3) to lock into their resting closed state. Acute burning typically drops by 50% to 70% during this window.</p>
						<p><strong>Phase 3 (Weeks 6 to 12): Central Desensitization and Tissue Remodeling:</strong> Once the peripheral volley of alarms is permanently halted and exposed root collars are sealed with physical resin varnishes, second-order neurons in the brainstem Subnucleus Caudalis drop out of hyperexcitable central wind-up, restoring a durable, pain-free baseline.</p>

