							<div class="backcover-header">Clinical Inquiry &bull; Patient Advocacy</div>
							<h2 class="backcover-tagline">What If Acidity Is Your Main&nbsp;Trigger?</h2>
							<p class="backcover-blurb">
								In our community poll of 77 Burning Mouth Syndrome patients, 70% reported that acidic food and drinks directly trigger or worsen their burning pain. That single clinical clue is the foundation of this&nbsp;investigation.
							</p>
							<p class="backcover-blurb">
								This is grounded in 21st-century biophysics. Dental pulpal nerves are densely packed with <strong>Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASICs)</strong>, first mapped in milestone 2004 neurobiology studies. When the atomic 3D crystal structure of ASICs was solved in 2014, it revealed the physical mechanism: extracellular acid protons dock into an open gate, firing high-voltage nerve telemetry even when oral tissue looks completely pink and&nbsp;healthy.
							</p>

							<div class="backcover-figure">
								<img src="/acid-sensing-ion-channels-asic-nerve.jpg" alt="Technical diagram of Acid-Sensing Ion Channels (ASIC) transducing acid protons into trigeminal burning pain signals based on 2004 pulpal mapping and 2014 crystal structure" loading="eager" />
							</div>

							<ul class="backcover-features">
								<li><strong>Groundbreaking Receptor Science:</strong> How 2004 pulpal nerve discoveries and 2014 atomic crystal structures prove that acid physically gates open Trigeminal pain&nbsp;channels.</li>
								<li><strong>The 70% Acidity Benchmark:</strong> Why tomatoes, citrus, vinegars, coffee, and sodas repeatedly provoke severe symptom&nbsp;spikes.</li>
								<li><strong>The 72-Hour Fermentation Clock:</strong> Why Monday's fermentable starches brew subgingival lactic acid that peaks in a flare on Wednesday&nbsp;afternoon.</li>
